import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

// Data shape: count: number
export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Runs after the first render and after every render where count changed.
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `Clicked ${count} times`;
    // No cleanup: this effect starts nothing.
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount((value) => value + 1)}>
      Clicked {count} times
    </button>
  );
}

body:
The hook needs two things to be correct: a callback that only touches the outside world, and an array listing every value the callback reads from the component.

### The three array shapes

```jsx
useEffect(() => { /* once after mount */ }, []);
useEffect(() => { /* mount and when id changes */ }, [id]);
useEffect(() => { /* after every render, usually a bug */ });
```

React compares dependency values with `Object.is`. Objects, arrays and functions declared during render are new references on every render, so they make the effect run every time. Move them out of the component, wrap them in `useMemo` or `useCallback`, or declare the function inside the effect.

If a value can be computed during render, compute it there. An effect that copies a prop into state is one render behind and doubles the work.

example:
// Run once, on mount. Correct for an initial load with no parameters.
useEffect(() => {
  loadHotels();
}, []);

// Run again when the route parameter changes.
useEffect(() => {
  loadBookings(id);
}, [id]);

// Stale closure: the effect runs once and keeps the first city it ever saw.
// The header updates, the list does not.
useEffect(() => {
  loadHotels(city);
}, []); // wrong

body:
Every component value the callback reads belongs in the array. The lint rule `exhaustive-deps` exists for this and it is usually right.

A missing dependency produces a stale closure: the effect holds the values from the render that created it. The visible symptom is a list for the previous id, a total that only refreshes after a click, or a counter stuck at its first value.

An extra dependency is rarely fatal but it re-runs the effect more often than needed. A new object literal in the array is the common case:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  load({ page, word });
}, [{ page, word }]); // new object every render, runs every render
```

Pass the fields instead: `[page, word]`.

example:
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setTick((n) => n + 1), 1000);

  // Called before the next run and on unmount. Without it the timer keeps
  // running on a component that no longer exists.
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);

